## Goal
Do the six samples separate by treatment? Compute a sample-level PCA from the gene counts and draw one plot of PC1 vs PC2, with samples coloured treated versus untreated.

## Inputs
Artifact types resolve through each sub-stage's OUTPUTS.tsv at execution time; the paths
below are what they resolve to today.

| Artifact type | Resolved from | Path |
|---|---|---|
| counts_gene | 02_bioinformatics/rnaseq_bulk/01_nfcore-rnaseq-wrapper (native) | 02_bioinformatics/rnaseq_bulk/01_nfcore-rnaseq-wrapper/run/results/star_salmon/salmon.merged.gene_counts_length_scaled.tsv |
| design | 01_prepare_samplesheets (native) | 01_samplesheets/rnaseq_bulk_design.csv |

## Method
Environment: `gars-bio` (pandas, numpy, scikit-learn, matplotlib). Every step runs inside one
script, `scripts/sample_pca.py`, submitted through the executor. Inputs are read-only.

1. Read the `counts_gene` table (genes x samples; `gene_id` and `gene_name` are annotation
   columns, the remaining columns are samples) and the `design` table. Require that the sample
   columns and the design's `sample_id` values are the same set (6 samples); stop with an error
   otherwise.
2. Transform: `log2(count + 1)`. The counts are salmon length-scaled gene counts, so no further
   length normalisation is applied.
3. Drop genes with zero variance across the samples, and keep all remaining genes (no
   top-N selection). Record the number kept.
4. Centre each gene (mean zero across samples); do not scale to unit variance.
5. PCA with `sklearn.decomposition.PCA(n_components=min(n_samples, n_genes))`, `svd_solver="full"`.
6. Plot PC1 (x) against PC2 (y), one point per sample, coloured by the design's `condition`
   (`treated`, `untreated`), each point labelled with its `sample_id`, axis labels carrying the
   percent variance explained, a legend for the two conditions. Colours are distinguishable
   without relying on hue alone (different marker shapes per condition). Save as PNG at 300 dpi
   (`results/sample_pca.png`) and as vector PDF (`results/sample_pca.pdf`).

